WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.658 --> 00:00:07.200
SEAN-MICHAEL LEWIS: Hello, and
welcome to my talk Do it live!

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.712
Fitbit's Zero-Downtime
Migration to GCP.

00:00:10.712 --> 00:00:11.670
I'm Sean-Michael Lewis.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.670
I'm Principal Software
Engineer at Fitbit.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.550
And I was in charge of moving
our monolithic application

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.485
from our legacy managed hosting
to Google Cloud platform.

00:00:21.485 --> 00:00:23.860
In today's talk, I'm going
to talk about what was unique

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.850
or what we felt was unique about
Fitbit's migration challenge.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.320
We are going to
talk about how we

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.650
thought about users
in making decisions

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.907
to move for our migration.

00:00:35.907 --> 00:00:37.990
Then we'll talk about the
technology and processes

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:41.520
that we added and affected
to make this happen.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.270
We'll talk a little
bit about how it went,

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.283
which was quite well.

00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:45.700
And then we'll
have some takeaways

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.590
that we think could
be useful for you,

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.740
should you be moving from
another cloud provider

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:55.600
or your own managed hosting
into Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.990
So, like many companies,
Fitbit started

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.400
with a monolithic application.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.300
We had a single binary
that did all the things.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.820
It is quite large.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.080
It handles all of our traffic,
or it did for a long time.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.470
And then, we started to
break it up into what

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.460
we call microservices.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.550
It's pretty standard
when a company matures.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:19.052
But while moving microservices
might be quite easy,

00:01:19.052 --> 00:01:21.510
each service can do its own
thing, move its own data store,

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.260
figure things out and
move into the cloud,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.140
a monolith is much
more challenging.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:31.010
It's more challenging,
it's bigger, meatier,

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.600
it takes up about
70% of our traffic.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.370
So moving it was quite hard.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:40.620
So our migration wasn't going to
happen until the monolith move.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.450
So let's talk
about our monolith.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.850
Like I said, it's a
single Java binary,

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:50.590
about 1,000 instances backed
by around 200 MySQL instances.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.900
Those MySQL sequences are
mostly sharded by user data.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.450
So if a request comes in,
I can get all of my data

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.090
from one shard.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.700
We have a sharding library
that manages the shards.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.900
So it figures out where users
go when new users sign up.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.380
It allows you to
expand and contract

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.160
the number of MySQL
sequences we have.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.320
And it also handles
caching, for which we had

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.550
around 400 nodes of mem cache.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.220
We also have Kafka, where we
do asynchronous processing.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.950
And those messages are largely
processed by other instances

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.850
within the monolith.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.532
So we'll talk about--

00:02:29.532 --> 00:02:33.790
yeah, we'll talk
about our users now.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.060
Now that we know what the
application looks like,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.550
we thought about what it
would look like for us to move

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.340
and who we should be thinking
about as we're moving.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.023
The most important stakeholder
in the move is your users.

00:02:45.023 --> 00:02:46.940
Sometimes, doing the
best thing for your users

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.600
is not the most easy
thing technically.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.640
But that should be the
thing you do first,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.280
or the person you
think about first.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.647
So, how do we get to GCP
with our users in mind?

00:02:56.647 --> 00:02:58.730
We thought about two
different ways of doing this.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.650
One was to do it progressively,
so move user by user,

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.850
or batch of user
by batch of user.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.900
Or we could do it all at once.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.790
There are different
pros and cons to each.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.610
For the progressive, we
thought that anything bad that

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.220
happened in the new
environment as we were moving,

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.370
any new challenge that we
experienced in our new home

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.950
would be limited to only
the users that have moved.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.090
So it should be a smaller
impact overall to our user base

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.210
as we figured out things
in the new environment.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:32.797
Secondly, outside of normal
availability problems,

00:03:32.797 --> 00:03:35.380
just how our application tended
to load in the new environment

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.960
would be able to be observed
in a more gradual fashion.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:43.090
We could see how the
environment dealt with new load

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.730
in a more measured way.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.860
We knew that network
calls-- we wouldn't

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.460
be able to make every network
call be in the new data

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:52.698
center in GCP.

00:03:52.698 --> 00:03:55.240
So we would have to go back to
the old data center sometimes,

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.160
and that would incur
a lot of latency.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:58.820
That's not a great
user experience,

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.690
but it's one of the
downsides of this approach.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:06.980
And then lastly, we'd have to
operate two full application

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.880
stacks simultaneously,
be able to operate them

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.170
at the same time and manage them
at the same time in two data

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.589
centers and two different
hosting [INAUDIBLE] for us.

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:20.943
On the contrary, the
all-at-once solution--

00:04:20.943 --> 00:04:23.110
any challenges you have in
the new environment, when

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.820
you cut over to the
new environment,

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:25.910
it affects everyone.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:30.400
So if you have a big
outage, everyone's down.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.640
It's basically a
total system shock.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.895
There's a lot of risk in that.

00:04:34.895 --> 00:04:36.520
You can do a lot of
testing beforehand,

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.520
but you don't know what's
really going to happen

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.707
until you flip the switch.

00:04:39.707 --> 00:04:42.040
The positive is that once
you're in the new data center,

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:43.790
all of your network
calls are local,

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:47.380
so your latency situation is
as good as it's going to be.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.930
And while you don't have to
maintain and operate two data

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.210
centers at the
same time, you need

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.010
the old one on standby
in case something

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.160
goes wrong in the move.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.400
So you're not really
getting rid of the old one

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.940
when you cut over, at least
until you're fully in.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.800
So, which one did we choose?

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:06.920
We felt that overall,
thinking of the users in mind,

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:10.640
that the progressive
migration was going

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.200
to be much better for them.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.020
On average, most users wouldn't
experience any downtime

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.320
or any noticeable downtime.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:19.220
That took some
work, but we thought

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.940
that if we could
slowly roll into GCP,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.433
we would give our users
the best experience,

00:05:25.433 --> 00:05:26.975
and maybe even one
they don't notice.

00:05:29.615 --> 00:05:30.990
So, as we were
thinking about how

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.948
we were going to do this
progressive migration,

00:05:32.948 --> 00:05:36.370
we had to think about, what are
the things we want out of it?

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:37.580
How are we going to do it?

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.000
What were going to be
our guiding principles?

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.990
So, the first one was to make
our best effort to route users

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.750
to the environment where
their data is resident.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.780
As I said, we shard
our data out by user.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:53.970
So, if I move my data from
our old hosting to GCP,

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.300
I should start routing
my requests to GCP.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.200
That was the goal of this
migration, is to move

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.630
the user with the data.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.490
We wanted to make
sure that-- let's say

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.350
we didn't route correctly
in my data is in GCP,

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:10.510
but I got routed to
the old data center.

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:12.850
Well, that should still work.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:14.640
We still want that
to be an experience.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.640
We don't want to be throwing
errors when we have a misroute.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.570
So we want an experience
no matter what.

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:22.380
We want to be able
to move users to GCP,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:23.838
but if things aren't
going well, we

00:06:23.838 --> 00:06:25.940
need to be able
to move them back.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.920
And we also need to be able
to move at variable speed.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:30.720
If things are going
great, we should

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.380
be able to move
users more quickly.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.380
If they're not going so
well, or we're not sure,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:38.610
we should trickle them in and
get our sea legs, as it were.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:41.030
Lastly, as we're making
these decisions about fast,

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.870
slow, forward,
backwards, we don't

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.588
want to be making a lot
of gut-check decisions.

00:06:45.588 --> 00:06:47.130
We want to be able
to look at a chart

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.930
and say we're
meeting in our SLOs.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.550
We're going to have
to move forward now.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.797
And so, we actually needed
to develop more SLOs

00:06:53.797 --> 00:06:56.130
and have a better handle on
them before we could do this

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:57.168
appropriately.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.930
Let's talk about the
timeline for this migration.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.530
So as I said, we wanted
to move users slow

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.190
and quickly, at
different varying times.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.600
But before that, we needed
to actually set everything up

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.900
in GCP and make
sure it was working.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.650
And you can always run tests,
but until you're really

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:19.390
routing production users
there, it's not real.

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:22.170
So, the first users that
we routed were employees.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.510
We routed them through GCP.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.770
All their data was still
in our old data center.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.460
They had slow experiences,
but at the beginning,

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.542
a lot of things were
broken, but we fixed bugs

00:07:31.542 --> 00:07:33.000
until we had a
complete experience.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.417
And all the
meaningful experiences

00:07:34.417 --> 00:07:39.240
that happened in Fitbit
were serviceable by going

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:45.010
through GCP, and even if data
was in another data center.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.392
Once we felt
confident in that, we

00:07:47.392 --> 00:07:49.600
decided it was time to start
moving paying customers.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.990
So we started moving
users slowly--

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:53.380
in a slow trickle.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.240
When we got to
watches, our systems

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.040
took on that new
traffic and made sure

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:58.880
that we were able to
operate and monitor

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:04.120
and really made sure that
things were working in GCP.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:05.900
Then, once we got a
lot of confidence,

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.380
we started moving
users in big batches.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.270
We moved them quickly.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:15.730
And as you'll see, quicker
than we even anticipated.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:18.820
So, how did we change d
architecture and technology

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.220
to make this kind
of move possible?

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:24.910
As you'll see on the left,
I have the same diagram

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:29.020
for our monolithic architecture,
for our legacy data center.

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:31.180
We replicated that in
Google Cloud Platform,

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.360
set the whole thing up.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.150
We had fewer applications
at the start and fewer MySQL

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.750
instances.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.820
And those MySQL
instances were empty

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.760
when we started as well because
they had no data in them.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.362
There was no data in GCP yet.

00:08:45.362 --> 00:08:47.570
And then, we had connections
from all the application

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:51.140
instances to all the MySQLs,
in both the old data center

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.190
and in GCP.

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:54.680
So it fully connected
between those two data

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:56.750
centers between
application instances.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:58.250
Those are the blue
lines you'll see.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:01.960
The application
instances go to--

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:03.710
those are the lines
that we had to add.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.918
We had to add connections
back to the old data center

00:09:05.918 --> 00:09:09.530
from GCP, and to GCP
from the old data center.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:11.070
Then we added edge
routing as well.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:14.030
This was the logic that
would allow us to route users

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:15.470
based on where they lived.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.650
So we had to be able to
push that logic somewhere.

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:20.900
It turns out we used
Cloudflare, which

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:25.340
is a CDN and DNS provider.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.733
They had a product
called Cloudflare Workers

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:29.150
that we were able
to push logic up

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:30.755
into and make these decisions.

00:09:30.755 --> 00:09:32.503
And I'll talk more
about that later.

00:09:32.503 --> 00:09:33.920
But that made it
so that we didn't

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.110
have to go into one data
center to decide that we really

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:37.860
wanted to service it
out of the other one,

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.560
and kick it back, and redirect.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:44.430
It made everything much cleaner
and easier to observe as well.

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:47.650
And then, lastly, we have
connectivity between our mem

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:49.530
cache instances.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.010
You'll notice that the
app, in this diagram,

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:55.070
the applications only talk to
their data center local mem

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:59.630
cached, but if one application--
if one data center makes

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.820
changes to its
local mem cache, we

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.090
want that change to be
replicated to the other one.

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:08.110
And so, we introduced
a proxy that

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:10.920
would allow us to echo in
validations to the other data

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:11.432
center.

00:10:11.432 --> 00:10:12.640
I'll talk about that as well.

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:18.030
First, we'll talk
about moving our users.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.760
I said, we had,
initially, empty databases

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.400
in GCP and all
our full databases

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.220
in our legacy data center.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:30.030
In our sharding scheme, we
have this notion of buckets.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:32.250
Buckets have users in them.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:36.990
Mostly, there's only really
two ways to do sharding.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:39.750
There's buckets and
there's ID ranges.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.300
We used buckets as kind
of like a hashing scheme.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:45.690
But it allows us to move
an individual bucket

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:46.380
one at a time.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:49.720
So we can move one bucket to
any other database server.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.390
So when we were
moving into GCP, we

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:53.520
could move one bucket from
a database server that

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.560
lived in our old
data center, and then

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:59.040
move it to GCP now that
users have been moved.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:00.750
This is the slow
method of movement.

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:02.458
It takes about 20
minutes because there's

00:11:02.458 --> 00:11:03.930
a lot of locking involved.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:05.910
Users can still read
their data, but they can't

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.620
write for that 20 minutes.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:09.010
It's slow and gradual.

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:11.790
We can add parallelism to get
a little bit more speed so we

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:13.890
can do many buckets at a time.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:20.270
But we can't really move
tons of users at once.

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:22.840
To move fast, move
the whole shard.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:26.940
And this is a typical
replication strategy,

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:28.817
which is that our
leader database would

00:11:28.817 --> 00:11:29.900
be in our old data center.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:33.520
We'd have a replica
of it in GCP.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:37.370
At some point, we would flip
who the leader was and make

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:39.080
the leader the one in GCP.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.900
Now, all the users on that
shard, all those buckets

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:43.740
have shifted all at once.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:44.990
And that is a very fast thing.

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:46.940
It takes about five minutes.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:48.657
It can go back and forward.

00:11:48.657 --> 00:11:50.240
Both of them are
bi-directional, which

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:51.890
is important for our use case.

00:11:54.668 --> 00:11:56.210
I'll talk a little
bit about routing.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:59.420
As I said, Cloudflare
Workers is the technology

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:05.960
we used to provide some
logic as to where our users'

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:07.620
traffic should be routed.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:11.142
So, we have this idea of
buckets, as I told you about.

00:12:11.142 --> 00:12:12.600
There's a finite
number of buckets.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.660
In fact, the number
was finite enough

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:16.820
that we could make
a big map of it

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:22.760
and push that map in
a JavaScript binary

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:24.803
up to this Cloudflare Worker.

00:12:24.803 --> 00:12:27.220
We could also put a whitelist
in there, which allowed us--

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.840
it was how we were
able to route employees

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.790
before paying customers.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:34.940
So regardless of where
your data is resident,

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:36.770
we could say, oh, this
user should always

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.620
go to GCP no matter what, no
matter where their data is--

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:43.140
otherwise, see where
their bucket lives.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:44.730
So this is the flow diagram.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:46.940
We have timed
deployments of the logic.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:48.950
The code that executes
the logic, but also

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:53.450
the data that feeds
that logic gets time

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:56.330
deployed into Cloudflare.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:59.420
Then the logic is simply, is
the user in the whitelist?

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:01.880
If so, send them to GCP.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:03.800
If not, check where
their bucket lives.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:05.030
Is the bucket in GCP?

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:06.890
If yes, send them to GCP.

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:08.630
If not, or we have
some kind of error,

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.593
or there is no user
information, send them

00:13:11.593 --> 00:13:14.380
to the legacy data center.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:17.620
Eventually, once we had more
than 50% of movement into GCP,

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:19.460
we made the default GCP.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:25.970
The last technical aspect was
keeping the caches coherent.

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:29.800
As I said, applications only
talk to their local caches.

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:31.490
And if the other
data center changes

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.200
the cache on their
side, you want it

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.220
to be reflected in both places.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.425
So we introduced a new
piece of technology

00:13:37.425 --> 00:13:40.490
[INAUDIBLE] router and open
source mem cache proxy that

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:45.480
has the ability to echo
deletes and other operations

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:49.313
to other data centers, or other
clusters of mem cache nodes.

00:13:49.313 --> 00:13:51.980
Our second cluster just happened
to be at the other data center.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:58.090
So, this was pretty
challenging for us.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:13:59.865
Although it's pretty
straightforward

00:13:59.865 --> 00:14:02.990
and it's a good tool, we don't
think a lot about it now.

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.580
It was a stark difference
from implementing the routing

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:06.600
from before.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.250
The routing, being
a managed service,

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:10.820
ended up being really
easy for us to do.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:13.740
It gave us very few headaches,
we had very few incidents

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:14.780
around it.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.340
Whereas, introducing a
new piece of technology

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:20.280
just before you make a big
move was much more challenging

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:21.660
and ended up working out for us.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.983
But it started to show us
the differences between what

00:14:24.983 --> 00:14:27.150
might be possible when we
have more managed services

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:27.817
at our disposal.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.660
So, how did this all go?

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.880
It actually went quite well,
better than, I think, any of us

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:36.380
expected.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:42.290
The routing of employees
before everyone else

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:43.790
uncovered many critical bugs.

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:47.030
So it uncovered around 60 bugs.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:50.860
We closed 58 of them before
we moved to a paying customer.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.960
And I think those other two
got closed as a won't fix.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:56.330
They didn't end up
being that critical.

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:58.610
So really, everything
that was really

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:01.712
blocking an experience
in GCP was closed

00:15:01.712 --> 00:15:02.920
before we were able to do it.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:04.460
And we did that live.

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:05.840
We did it in production.

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:07.490
I mean, we tested
it in production

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:10.470
without affecting
our paying customers,

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.710
which was super awesome.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.100
And then, we also
finished early.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:19.470
We had a pretty hard deadline
to be out at a certain time.

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:20.760
And we started this process--

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:22.800
if you look at the
left of this graph,

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.190
you'll see that
there's a very slow,

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:29.500
a very low trickle of
throughput going to GCP,

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:30.500
which is the green line.

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:33.740
And you'll see that it
slowly steps up over time.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:37.980
And then around the end of
August, or 8-30 on this chart,

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:39.590
you'll see it takes
a big step up.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:42.590
And then it takes some other
bigger steps up and stays high.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:49.220
Based on our SLOs, we were able
to see that we were doing well,

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:51.110
everything was kind
of under control,

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:55.790
and then we could really push
this migration faster, and get

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:58.070
done sooner than we
expected, which was

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.940
super exciting for everyone.

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:04.240
So, what are the takeaways
from this process?

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:07.420
Progressive migration is
based on user data locality.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:09.940
It can provide the best user
experience for your users

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:10.720
most of the time.

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:15.730
If you have a sharded
monolith like we do,

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:19.510
you probably have some sort
of user sharding or something

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:20.730
like that.

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:22.670
And so, if you can do
it little by little,

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:25.330
that's going to be the
best bang for your buck.

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:29.260
Your movement should
have options, both speed

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:30.158
and direction.

00:16:30.158 --> 00:16:32.200
It gives you flexibility
for when things go wrong

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.870
or things go really well.

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:39.310
Decide what is really good or
really well based on your SLOs.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:43.210
And then also, we found that
this was the first opportunity

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:49.330
for us having been in our own
colo to use managed services

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:51.053
and really see the
benefit of them.

00:16:51.053 --> 00:16:52.720
Now that we're in
Google Cloud Platform,

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.428
we think there's a
lot more opportunities

00:16:54.428 --> 00:16:55.790
for us to do that.

00:16:55.790 --> 00:16:59.430
So, if there are opportunities
to use them in your migration,

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:03.860
I would say you should.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:04.503
That's all.

00:17:04.503 --> 00:17:05.920
Thank you very
much for your time.

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:08.919
And I hope your migration
goes well as well.

